              %p
                chapter 10:
                %em Quantum Shannon Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.07450"}> arXiv:1604.07450
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Quotes Quotes
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Fuchs"} Christopher Fuchs
            recounts (in an email from Dec. 1997, reproduced on
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1405.2390.pdf#page=350"} p. 292
            of “
            %em>
              %a{:href => "Christopher+Fuchs#FuchsSchlosshauerStacey14"}> My Struggles with the Block Universe
              “
            , 2014):
          %blockquote
            %p
              a little anecdote about
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Wheeler"} John Wheeler
              that I heard from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"} John Preskill
              a few days ago. In 1972 he had Wheeler for his freshman classical mechanics course at Princeton.
            %p One day Wheeler had each student write all the equations of physics s/he knew on a single sheet of paper. He gathered the papers up and placed them all side-by-side on the stage at the front of the classroom. Finally, he looked out at the students and said,
            %blockquote
              %p “These pages likely contain all the fundamental equations we know of physics. They encapsulate all that’s known of the world.”
            %p Then he looked at the papers and said,
            %blockquote
              %p “Now fly!”
            %p Nothing happened. He looked out at the audience, then at the papers, raised his hands high, and commanded,
            %blockquote
              %p “Fly!”
            %p Everyone was silent, thinking this guy had gone off his rocker. Wheeler said,
            %blockquote
              %p “You see, these equations can’t fly. But our universe flies. We’re still missing the single, simple ingredient that makes it all fly.”
          %p On this point, in the video documentary
          %ul
            %li
              Timothy Ferris,
              %em Creation of the Universe
              (1985) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0204224"}> imdb:tt0204224
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Wheeler"} John Wheeler
            appears (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Blake+Stacey"} Blake Stacey
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://www.sunclipse.org/?m=201601"} Jan 2016
          %blockquote
            %p There’s nothing deader than an equation. You write that down in a square on a tile floor. And on another tile on the floor you write down another equation, which you think might be a better description of the Universe. And you keep on writing down equations hoping to get a better and better equation for what the Universe is and does.
            %p And then, when you’ve worked your way out to the end of the room and have to step out, you wave your wand and tell the equations to fly.
            %p And not one of them will put on wings and fly.
            %p Yet the Universe flies!
            %p It has a life to it that no equation has, and that life to it is a life with which we are also tied up.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"} John Preskill
            re-enacting the episode at the conference banquet of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://events.perimeterinstitute.ca/event/43/"} It from QBit 2023
          %center
            %a{:href => "https://twitter.com/postquantum/status/1687080635012112384"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/OppenheimOnPreskillOnWheelerOnEquations.jpg"}/
          %p
            %br/
